Q: Accessing postgres db using pgxpool in a goroutine A: <p>Usually, you would create a single Postgres connection pool somewhere early in your app's startup code. Then you pass the connection pool as a dependency to your request handlers. Or to whatever abstractions you are using inside your request handlers, like repositories.</p>
<p>This approach works with the <a href="https://github.com/labstack/echo" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Echo</a> framework just as well.</p>
<p>For example, you could create a request handler like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>type GreetingHandler struct {
    db *pgxpool.Pool
}

func NewGreetingHandler(db *pgxpool.Pool) *GreetingHandler {
    return &amp;GreetingHandler{db: db}
}

func (h *GreetingHandler) Handle(c echo.Context) error {
    ctx := context.Background()
    greeting := &quot;&quot;
    err := h.db.QueryRow(ctx, &quot;SELECT 'Hello, world!'&quot;).Scan(&amp;greeting)
    if err != nil {
        return c.String(http.StatusInternalServerError, err.Error())
    }
    return c.String(http.StatusOK, greeting)
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>GreetingHandler</code> takes a <code>pgxpool.Pool</code> as a dependency. When handling a request in a goroutine, it calls <code>h.db.QueryRow</code>, which internally acquires a connection from the pool and executes the query.</p>
<p>Then you could wire the <code>GreetingHandler</code> to your Postgres connection pool and Echo instance as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>func main() {
    ctx := context.TODO()

    // Create a connection pool early before setting up request handlers.
    pool, err := pgxpool.New(ctx, os.Getenv(&quot;DB_URL&quot;))
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer pool.Close()

    // Make sure the database is reachable.
    if err := pool.Ping(ctx); err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    // Pass the connection pool as a dependency to the request handler.
    greeting := NewGreetingHandler(pool)

    // Register the request handlers to an Echo server, then start the server.
    e := echo.New()
    e.GET(&quot;/hello&quot;, greeting.Handle)
    if err := e.Start(&quot;:8080&quot;); err != http.ErrServerClosed {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to handle staking transactions using an existing deposit/withdraw crypto transaction DTO in Spring Boot (Kotlin) A: <p>I've implemented staking on top of existing deposit/withdraw flows in a similar architecture. Here's a practical approach that minimizes changes while keeping your code clean.</p>
<p>1. How should staking be represented in the DTO?</p>
<p>Yes, <code>operation = &quot;STAKE&quot;</code> (and <code>&quot;UNSTAKE&quot;</code>) is the right approach. Your existing DTO structure is flexible enough—you just need to establish clear conventions for how the fields map to staking semantics.</p>
<p><strong>Add these operation types:</strong></p>
<pre><code>object TransactionOperations {
    const val DEPOSIT = &quot;DEPOSIT&quot;
    const val WITHDRAW = &quot;WITHDRAW&quot;
    const val STAKE = &quot;STAKE&quot;
    const val UNSTAKE = &quot;UNSTAKE&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Staking <em>is</em> conceptually an internal transfer (user wallet → staking pool), but I'd recommend treating it as a distinct operation type rather than overloading the transfer logic. This keeps your conditional logic readable and audit trails clear.</p>
<p>2. Differentiating deposit/withdraw vs staking</p>
<p>Use <strong><code>operation</code> as the primary discriminator</strong>, with <code>source</code>/<code>destination</code> providing context:</p>
<pre><code>// Clean pattern using sealed class or enum
enum class TransactionType {
    DEPOSIT,    // external → user vault
    WITHDRAW,   // user vault → external
    STAKE,      // user vault → staking pool (internal)
    UNSTAKE     // staking pool → user vault (internal)
}

// In your service layer
fun processTransaction(dto: UserCryptoTransactionDto) {
    when (dto.data?.operation) {
        TransactionOperations.DEPOSIT -&gt; handleDeposit(dto)
        TransactionOperations.WITHDRAW -&gt; handleWithdraw(dto)
        TransactionOperations.STAKE -&gt; handleStake(dto)
        TransactionOperations.UNSTAKE -&gt; handleUnstake(dto)
        else -&gt; throw UnsupportedOperationException(&quot;Unknown operation: ${dto.data?.operation}&quot;)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>3. Staking payload example</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;CRYPTO_TRANSACTION&quot;,
  &quot;tenantId&quot;: &quot;tenant-123&quot;,
  &quot;timestamp&quot;: 1735340400000,
  &quot;data&quot;: {
    &quot;id&quot;: &quot;stake-uuid-001&quot;,
    &quot;transactionId&quot;: &quot;tx-stake-12345&quot;,
    &quot;userId&quot;: 42,
    &quot;vaultAccountId&quot;: &quot;vault-user-42&quot;,
    &quot;assetId&quot;: &quot;ETH&quot;,
    &quot;customerRefId&quot;: &quot;user-stake-request-001&quot;,

    &quot;source&quot;: {
      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;VAULT_ACCOUNT&quot;,
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;vault-user-42&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;User Main Wallet&quot;
    },
    &quot;sourceAddress&quot;: null,

    &quot;destination&quot;: {
      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;STAKING_POOL&quot;,
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;eth-staking-pool-01&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;ETH 2.0 Staking Pool&quot;
    },
    &quot;destinationAddress&quot;: null,

    &quot;amount&quot;: &quot;1.5&quot;,
    &quot;amountUSD&quot;: &quot;2850.00&quot;,
    &quot;networkFee&quot;: &quot;0&quot;,
    &quot;feeCurrency&quot;: &quot;ETH&quot;,

    &quot;status&quot;: &quot;PENDING&quot;,
    &quot;subStatus&quot;: &quot;PENDING_STAKE_CONFIRMATION&quot;,
    &quot;operation&quot;: &quot;STAKE&quot;,
    &quot;txHash&quot;: null,

    &quot;createdAt&quot;: 1735340400000,
    &quot;lastUpdated&quot;: 1735340400000,
    &quot;isActive&quot;: true
  },
  &quot;jsonData&quot;: null
}
</code></pre>
<p>For unstaking:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;CRYPTO_TRANSACTION&quot;,
  &quot;tenantId&quot;: &quot;tenant-123&quot;,
  &quot;timestamp&quot;: 1735340400000,
  &quot;data&quot;: {
    &quot;id&quot;: &quot;stake-uuid-001&quot;,
    &quot;transactionId&quot;: &quot;tx-stake-12345&quot;,
    &quot;userId&quot;: 42,
    &quot;vaultAccountId&quot;: &quot;vault-user-42&quot;,
    &quot;assetId&quot;: &quot;ETH&quot;,
    &quot;customerRefId&quot;: &quot;user-stake-request-001&quot;,

    &quot;source&quot;: {
      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;VAULT_ACCOUNT&quot;,
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;vault-user-42&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;User Main Wallet&quot;
    },
    &quot;sourceAddress&quot;: null,

    &quot;destination&quot;: {
      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;STAKING_POOL&quot;,
      &quot;id&quot;: &quot;eth-staking-pool-01&quot;,
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;ETH 2.0 Staking Pool&quot;
    },
    &quot;destinationAddress&quot;: null,

    &quot;amount&quot;: &quot;1.5&quot;,
    &quot;amountUSD&quot;: &quot;2850.00&quot;,
    &quot;networkFee&quot;: &quot;0&quot;,
    &quot;feeCurrency&quot;: &quot;ETH&quot;,

    &quot;status&quot;: &quot;PENDING&quot;,
    &quot;subStatus&quot;: &quot;PENDING_STAKE_CONFIRMATION&quot;,
    &quot;operation&quot;: &quot;STAKE&quot;,
    &quot;txHash&quot;: null,

    &quot;createdAt&quot;: 1735340400000,
    &quot;lastUpdated&quot;: 1735340400000,
    &quot;isActive&quot;: true
  },
  &quot;jsonData&quot;: null
}
</code></pre>
<h2>4. Service layer handling</h2>
<h3>Reuse the same transaction table</h3>
<p>Yes, reuse it. Add a column or two rather than a new table:</p>
<p>-- Minimal schema addition</p>
<p>ALTER TABLE crypto_transactions</p>
<p>ADD COLUMN staking_pool_id VARCHAR(64) NULL,</p>
<p>ADD COLUMN unlock_at TIMESTAMP NULL;  -- for unstaking cooldown periods</p>
<p>Balance handling: Lock, don't debit</p>
<p>This is the key insight. Staking should <strong>lock</strong> funds rather than remove them:</p>
<pre><code>@Service
class StakingService(
    private val transactionRepository: TransactionRepository,
    private val balanceService: BalanceService
) {
    @Transactional
    fun handleStake(dto: UserCryptoTransactionDto) {
        val data = dto.data ?: throw IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Missing transaction data&quot;)
        val amount = data.amount?.toBigDecimal() 
            ?: throw IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Missing amount&quot;)

        // 1. Validate available (unlocked) balance
        val available = balanceService.getAvailableBalance(
            userId = data.userId!!,
            assetId = data.assetId!!
        )
        if (available &lt; amount) {
            throw InsufficientBalanceException(&quot;Available: $available, requested: $amount&quot;)
        }

        // 2. Lock the balance (not debit)
        balanceService.lockBalance(
            userId = data.userId!!,
            assetId = data.assetId!!,
            amount = amount,
            reason = &quot;STAKE&quot;,
            referenceId = data.transactionId!!
        )

        // 3. Persist transaction record
        val entity = mapToEntity(dto).apply {
            stakingPoolId = data.destination?.id
            status = TransactionStatus.PENDING
        }
        transactionRepository.save(entity)

        // 4. Initiate staking with your provider (async recommended)
        stakingProviderClient.initiateStake(entity)
    }

    @Transactional
    fun handleUnstake(dto: UserCryptoTransactionDto) {
        val data = dto.data!!
        val amount = data.amount!!.toBigDecimal()

        // 1. Validate staked balance
        val staked = balanceService.getStakedBalance(data.userId!!, data.assetId!!)
        if (staked &lt; amount) {
            throw InsufficientStakedBalanceException()
        }

        // 2. Move from staked → pending_unlock (with cooldown)
        balanceService.initiateUnstake(
            userId = data.userId!!,
            assetId = data.assetId!!,
            amount = amount,
            unlockAt = Instant.now().plus(Duration.ofDays(21)) // ETH example
        )

        // 3. Persist
        val entity = mapToEntity(dto).apply {
            unlockAt = Instant.now().plus(Duration.ofDays(21))
        }
        transactionRepository.save(entity)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Balance model suggestion</p>
<pre><code>data class UserBalance(
    val userId: Long,
    val assetId: String,
    val total: BigDecimal,      // everything the user &quot;owns&quot;
    val available: BigDecimal,  // can withdraw/trade
    val locked: BigDecimal,     // pending operations
    val staked: BigDecimal,     // actively staking
    val pendingUnlock: BigDecimal // unstaking cooldown
) {
    init {
        require(total == available + locked + staked + pendingUnlock)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This approach has worked well in production—staking transactions flow through the same pipeline as deposits/withdrawals, share the same audit logging, and require minimal new infrastructure.</p>

Q: How do I get specific values from specific keys in dictionary? A: <p>Write a function that handles multiple parameters each of which is a dictionary. The function should be a generator.</p>
<p>For your first dictionary just pass that to the function.</p>
<p>For the list, just unpack the list and pass the result to this function.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>from collections.abc import Iterator

type Dt = dict[str, int]

epraise_points_per_form: Dt = {
    '7A': 5206, '7C': 4247, '7H': 5344, '7P': 4226,
    '8A': 4983, '8C': 4220, '8H': 4943, '8P': 4281, '8T': 5419,
    '9A': 3184, '9C': 4390, '9H': 3676, '9L': 4451, '9P': 3657, '9T': 5300,
    '10A': 4418, '10C': 3736, '10H': 5379, '10L': 4792, '10P': 4130, '10T': 4520,
    '11A': 2942, '11C': 3110, '11H': 2963, '11L': 2917, '11P': 2669, '11T': 3146,
    '12C': 1505, '12P': 1327, '12T': 1720,
    '13C': 1939, '13P': 2439, '13T': 2399
}

forms: list[Dt] = [
    {'7A': 5206, '7C': 4247, '7H': 5344, '7P': 4226},
    {'8A': 4983, '8C': 4220, '8H': 4943, '8P': 4281, '8T': 5419},
    {'9A': 3184, '9C': 4390, '9H': 3676, '9L': 4451, '9P': 3657, '9T': 5300,},
    {'10A': 4418, '10C': 3736, '10H': 5379, '10L': 4792, '10P': 4130, '10T': 4520,},
    {'11A': 2942, '11C': 3110, '11H': 2963, '11L': 2917, '11P': 2669, '11T': 3146,},
    {'12C': 1505, '12P': 1327, '12T': 1720},
    {'13C': 1939, '13P': 2439, '13T': 2399}
]

def get_values(*d_: Dt, prefix: str=&quot;7&quot;) -&gt; Iterator[int]:
    for d in d_:
        for k, v in d.items():
            if k.startswith(prefix):
                yield v

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    if values := list(get_values(epraise_points_per_form)):
        print(sum(values) / len(values))
    if values := list(get_values(*forms)):
        print(sum(values) / len(values))
</code></pre>

Q: select dropdown value and use in a sql select A: <p>Just get the value of assettypeid field on the post request, actually this is not done by JavaScript, it is by server-side.
And you don't need the onchange="typechanged();"</p>

<p>Sample code is as follow:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;c:when test="${empty param.assettypeid }"&gt;
    &lt;select name="assettypeid" id="assettypeid"&gt;

    &lt;sql:query var="queryresults" dataSource="jdbc/icantrack"&gt;
    SELECT id, name FROM assettype order by name
    &lt;/sql:query&gt;

     &lt;option value="1"&gt;-- All Asset Types--&lt;/option&gt;
     &lt;c:forEach var="row" items="${queryresults.rows}"&gt;
     &lt;option value="&lt;c:out value="${row.id}"/&gt;"
     &lt;c:if test="${row.id == param.assettypeid}"&gt;
          &lt;c:out value="selected"/&gt;
     &lt;/c:if&gt;
     &lt;c:out value="${row.name}" /&gt;
     &lt;/option&gt;
     &lt;/c:forEach&gt;
     &lt;/select&gt;
&lt;/c:when&gt;
&lt;c:otherwise&gt;
    Hello &lt;b&gt;&lt;c:out value="${param.assettypeid}" /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;!
&lt;/c:otherwise&gt;
</code></pre>
